      "thinking": "The real fault here is that the account page has a hard dependency on the payment provider with no graceful degradation, so when it returns 503s the whole page fails. The fix should render the page without card details, exposing a card_failure flag similar to cache_failure, instead of failing entirely; I also want to check whether the latency spike is just the slow provider or something else like retry logic amplifying it.\n\nI'll need to update the two existing tests that assert the old failure behaviour (checking figure_cents is None and a \"PaymentProviderFailed:\" message) since those assertions describe exactly the broken behaviour I'm intentionally changing \u2014 I'm allowed to rewrite them to assert graceful degradation instead. Now I'm sketching the serve_account_page logic: wrapping the payment lookup so a provider exception yields card=None and a card_failure message instead of aborting the whole request.\n\nI'm weighing whether the fetch helper should catch only PaymentProviderFailed or any Exception \u2014 network errors and timeouts from the provider seam probably deserve the same graceful treatment, but I want to be careful not to silently swallow real bugs, so recording the failure message rather than hiding it feels like the right balance.\n\nrecord_visit still runs on the success path either way, so I'll keep that recording in place regardless of card failure. I'll leave latency alone since we can't add timeouts here, and just add a new optional `card_failure: str | None = None` field to RenderedPage at the end for compatibility. Now I'm sketching the test: a downed provider should still yield a page with figure and basket intact, failure None, card_failure describing the host and 503, and card_last_four None.\n\nBefore finalizing, I should check whether other callers use RenderedPage or serve_account_page elsewhere, like in rollout.py or __init__.py, so I'll search for usages of serve_account_page.\n\n",
